    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 2, 2]  ->  [[], [1], [1, 2], [1, 2, 2], [2], [2, 2]]\nnums = [0]        ->  [[], [0]]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: sort, then skip equal neighbours at the same level"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Use the **loop-from-start** template: the node for a subset tries adding each number at or after `start`, and **every node is itself an answer**. Duplicates appear when two equal values are tried **at the same level**: taking the first 2 and taking the second 2 lead to identical subtrees. Sort first so equal values sit together, then at each level skip a value equal to the one just tried: `if (i > start && nums[i] === nums[i − 1]) continue`. Taking a 2 and then **another** 2 deeper down is still allowed — that's `i == start`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. All subsets, then remove repeats"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function subsetsWithDup(nums) {\n  const sorted = [...nums].sort((a, b) => a - b);   // so equal subsets look the same\n  const seen = new Set();\n  const result = [];\n  for (let mask = 0; mask < 1 << sorted.length; mask++) {\n    const s = sorted.filter((_, j) => mask & (1 << j));\n    const key = s.join(\",\");\n    if (!seen.has(key)) {\n      seen.add(key);\n      result.push(s);\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def subsets_with_dup(nums):\n    nums = sorted(nums)                   # so equal subsets look the same\n    seen, result = set(), []\n    for mask in range(1 << len(nums)):\n        s = tuple(nums[j] for j in range(len(nums)) if mask >> j & 1)\n        if s not in seen:\n            seen.add(s)\n            result.append(list(s))\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {\n    int[] a = nums.clone();\n    Arrays.sort(a);                       // so equal subsets look the same\n    Set<List<Integer>> seen = new LinkedHashSet<>();\n    for (int mask = 0; mask < 1 << a.length; mask++) {\n        List<Integer> s = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int j = 0; j < a.length; j++) if ((mask >> j & 1) == 1) s.add(a[j]);\n        seen.add(s);\n    }\n    return new ArrayList<>(seen);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "vector<vector<int>> subsetsWithDup(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> a = nums;\n    sort(a.begin(), a.end());             // so equal subsets look the same\n    set<vector<int>> seen;\n    for (int mask = 0; mask < 1 << (int)a.size(); mask++) {\n        vector<int> s;\n        for (int j = 0; j < (int)a.size(); j++) if (mask >> j & 1) s.push_back(a[j]);\n        seen.insert(s);\n    }\n    return vector<vector<int>>(seen.begin(), seen.end());\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · 2ⁿ), space O(n · 2ⁿ)** for the set. It builds every repeat only to throw it away."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Backtracking that never builds a repeat"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "subsets-dup",
      "caption": "Every node is a subset; a value equal to the one just tried at the same level is skipped."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function subsetsWithDup(nums) {\n  const a = [...nums].sort((x, y) => x - y);   // equal values next to each other\n  const result = [];\n  const path = [];\n  const build = (start) => {\n    result.push([...path]);                    // every node is a subset\n    for (let i = start; i < a.length; i++) {\n      if (i > start && a[i] === a[i - 1]) continue;   // same value at the same level: a repeat\n      path.push(a[i]);\n      build(i + 1);\n      path.pop();\n    }\n  };\n  build(0);\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def subsets_with_dup(nums):\n    a = sorted(nums)                      # equal values next to each other\n    result, path = [], []\n\n    def build(start):\n        result.append(path[:])            # every node is a subset\n        for i in range(start, len(a)):\n            if i > start and a[i] == a[i - 1]:\n                continue                  # same value at the same level: a repeat\n            path.append(a[i])\n            build(i + 1)\n            path.pop()\n\n    build(0)\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {\n    int[] a = nums.clone();\n    Arrays.sort(a);                              // equal values next to each other\n    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    build(a, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid build(int[] a, int start, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> result) {\n    result.add(new ArrayList<>(path));           // every node is a subset\n    for (int i = start; i < a.length; i++) {\n        if (i > start && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;   // same value at the same level: a repeat\n        path.add(a[i]);\n        build(a, i + 1, path, result);\n        path.remove(path.size() - 1);\n    }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void build(vector<int>& a, int start, vector<int>& path, vector<vector<int>>& result) {\n    result.push_back(path);                      // every node is a subset\n    for (int i = start; i < (int)a.size(); i++) {\n        if (i > start && a[i] == a[i - 1]) continue;   // same value at the same level: a repeat\n        path.push_back(a[i]);\n        build(a, i + 1, path, result);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> subsetsWithDup(vector<int>& nums) {\n    vector<int> a = nums;\n    sort(a.begin(), a.end());                    // equal values next to each other\n    vector<vector<int>> result;\n    vector<int> path;\n    build(a, 0, path, result);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n · 2ⁿ)** in the worst case (all distinct), **space O(n)** besides the output. With many duplicates the tree is much smaller than 2ⁿ, because the repeats are never built."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "All subsets, then remove repeats",
          "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "All **2ⁿ** subsets are built, each in O(n), even the repeats.",
          "space": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
          "spaceWhy": "The set stores **every subset**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Backtracking that never builds a repeat",
          "time": "O(n · 2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "2ⁿ at worst, and far fewer with duplicates: **repeats are never built**.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "One path and the call stack (besides the output)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**All equal** — `[2, 2, 2]`: `[], [2], [2,2], [2,2,2]`.",
        "**Unsorted input** — `[4, 4, 1, 4]`: sort first, or the skip check misses repeats.",
        "**`i > start`, not `i > 0`** — with `i > 0` you'd also forbid `[2, 2]`.",
        "**No duplicates** — it behaves like Subsets."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Combination Sum II** — the same skip, plus a target sum.",
        "**Permutations II** — duplicates when order matters: skip with a `used` check instead.",
        "**Subsets** — distinct values, no skip needed."
      ]
    }
  ],
